def is_valid(s: str) -> bool:
    # Initialize a stack to keep track of opening brackets
    stack = []
    # Dictionary to map closing brackets to their corresponding opening brackets
    bracket_map = {")": "(", "]": "[", "}": "{"}

    for char in s:
        if char in "([{":
            # Push opening brackets onto the stack
            stack.append(char)
        elif char == "*":
            # '*' can act as any bracket or nothing, so push all possibilities
            stack.extend(["(", ")", ""])
        else:
            # If stack is empty or top doesn't match the corresponding opening bracket, return False
            if not stack or (stack.pop() != bracket_map[char] and stack[-1] != ""):
                return False

    # Check if all opening brackets have been closed
    while stack:
        if stack.pop() != "":
            return False

    return True
```

This function uses a stack to keep track of opening brackets encountered. It iterates through each character in the input string. For every opening bracket, it pushes it onto the stack. For every closing bracket, it checks if the stack is empty or if the top of the stack does not match the corresponding opening bracket. The wildcard character `*` is handled by pushing all possible interpretations (`"("`, `")"`, `""`) onto the stack. Finally, it ensures that all opening brackets have been properly closed by checking if the stack is empty.
